# MK LABS — COMPLETE DIGITAL PRODUCTS STORE & ADMIN MANAGEMENT SYSTEM

## PROJECT OBJECTIVE

Build a complete, production-minded Digital Products Store and Admin Management System for the existing MK Labs website.

This must be a fully functional implementation, NOT a UI-only mockup, prototype, or collection of placeholder screens.

The system must allow the MK Labs administrator to:
- Create, edit, duplicate, publish, unpublish, archive, and delete digital products.
- Upload one ZIP bundle per product.
- Upload multiple individual files under one product.
- Use BOTH ZIP uploads and individual file uploads together in the same product.
- Upload product cover images and gallery images separately from downloadable files.
- Organize products using categories and tags.
- Manage free, paid-catalogue, external-link, and coming-soon products.
- Manage customers and their download permissions.
- Track product downloads and basic store activity.
- Manage public store content and settings.

The public website must allow visitors to:
- Browse the digital product catalogue.
- Search, filter, and sort products.
- View detailed product pages.
- See product descriptions, previews, included files, formats, and other information.
- Download products that are configured as free.
- Access external resources when a product uses an external link.
- See a clear notice when a product is listed as paid but purchasing is not yet available.

## CRITICAL PROJECT RULES

1. First inspect the existing project, its source code, framework, routing, components, styling, configuration, and deployment setup.
2. Extend the existing MK Labs website. Do not unnecessarily rebuild or replace it.
3. Preserve all existing pages, sections, content, branding, product links, and working functionality.
4. Preserve the existing product showcase and its external application links.
5. Keep existing FileBatch, Mekit, ImgKit, and Certificate Studio links working.
6. Do not break the existing responsive layout, navigation, contact section, or footer.
7. Follow the existing project's framework and coding conventions. Do not migrate to another framework without a strong technical reason.
8. Use reusable components, clean architecture, TypeScript where applicable, and centralized configuration.
9. Do not create fake functionality, fake statistics, fake customer records, fake downloads, fake reviews, fake orders, or fake revenue.
10. Every visible button, form, filter, and admin action must work.
11. Do not expose secret keys, private storage credentials, service-role keys, or unrestricted database access in frontend code.
12. Do not implement payment processing, checkout, cart, orders, subscriptions, or payment gateways.

**PAYMENT IS OUT OF SCOPE.** Do not install or integrate Stripe, PayPal, JazzCash, Easypaisa, or any other payment provider. The system may store optional catalogue pricing information for future use, but it must not accept payments or pretend that a purchase has taken place.

